# What a DO/LOOP header's values print while they are being computed.
#
# A header slot used to be one `Op::EvalExpr`: `eval.rs` for the whole
# expression, with the `>K>` echo and the validation as ops behind it. The
# expression is now its own native ops where `native_shape` accepts it, and
# `Op::LoopHeaderValue` is still what the slot owes -- so what moved is which
# code emits a value's intermediate trace lines, and where the `>K>` sits among
# them.
#
# The rows are transcripts: each is one program whose stdout, stderr and exit
# status are recorded byte for byte, so a line that appears, disappears or
# moves shows as a diff.
#
# **What this file catches was measured with the rest of the directory held
# out, and per row**, which is the only way to ask the question: the harness's
# own engine-vs-engine `assert_eq!` panics out of `datadriven::walk` at the
# first disagreeing stanza in any file, and `walk` visits files in
# `fs::read_dir` order, so which file -- and which row of it -- reports a
# shared failure is filesystem order and stanza order rather than anything
# about the tree.
#
# **Every row here reddens `both_engines_agree_on_every_case_file` on its own**
# under two mutations of the header's compilation: the keyword echo emitted in
# front of its slot's ops, and every slot resolving to slot 0. The call row
# catches two more that the other rows do not, and its own comment names them.
# The file stays green when the header stops compiling natively, which is the
# promotion's contract: the bytes do not move.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE, on both
# engines, before this file was written. Regenerate a row by running its
# program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 -- ir_dual.rs refuses to run
# at all under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty: an empty
# line ends an expected block, and a program's own output may hold one. A
# trailing blank inside a line is part of what is compared, and the tag is what
# keeps one away from the end of a line that would otherwise look empty.

# trace i over a TO/BY/FOR header, which is the `>K>` order per header value
# The interleave is the property: each value's own intermediate lines come
# first, then that value's `>K>`, then the next value is evaluated at all. The
# initial value has intermediate lines and no `>K>` of its own, and the TO
# bound is a symbol read rather than a literal, so the run of lines is not one
# shape repeated.
#
# Under the echo-position mutation the header describes, every `>K>` here reads
# a register nothing has written and prints "The NIL object".
program
trace i
zn = 6
do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
  say zi
end
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 3
err>      2 *-* zn = 6
err>        >L>   "6"
err>        >>>   "6"
err>        >=>   ZN <= "6"
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
err>        >L>   "1"
err>        >V>   ZN => "6"
err>        >K>   "TO" => "6"
err>        >L>   "2"
err>        >K>   "BY" => "2"
err>        >L>   "2"
err>        >K>   "FOR" => "2"
err>        >=>   ZI <= "1"
err>      4 *-*   say zi
err>        >V>     ZI => "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      5 *-* end
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
err>        >V>     ZI => "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>        >>>     "3"
err>        >=>     ZI <= "3"
err>      4 *-*   say zi
err>        >V>     ZI => "3"
err>        >>>     "3"
err>      5 *-* end
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
err>        >V>     ZI => "3"
err>        >>>     "3"
err>        >>>     "5"
err>        >=>     ZI <= "5"

# trace r over the same header, where the `>K>` lines are the whole of it
# The same values under a setting that prints no intermediate line at all, which
# is what separates the echo order from the values' own ops: `trace i` shows a
# `>K>` sitting among the lines its value produced, and this shows them adjacent
# and in the order the keywords were written.
#
# It catches the echo-position mutation with no intermediate line in the block
# to move, which is what makes it a different instrument from the row above
# rather than a shorter copy of it.
program
trace r
zn = 6
do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
  say zi
end
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 3
err>      2 *-* zn = 6
err>        >>>   "6"
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
err>        >K>   "TO" => "6"
err>        >K>   "BY" => "2"
err>        >K>   "FOR" => "2"
err>      4 *-*   say zi
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      5 *-* end
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
err>        >>>     "1"
err>        >>>     "3"
err>      4 *-*   say zi
err>        >>>     "3"
err>      5 *-* end
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to zn by 2 for 2
err>        >>>     "3"
err>        >>>     "5"

# trace i over a header whose bound is a call
# The call takes an `Op::CallExpr` addressed at slot 1 of the DO, which resolves
# only because `Interp::chunk_node_at` descends a header slot through
# `loop_header_slot`. The argument's `>A>` and the call's own `>F>` sit in front
# of the `>K>` the bound owes, so this row holds the address and the order at
# once.
#
# **The two the other rows do not catch**, each measured with this stanza as
# the only one in the directory, and each measured to leave the other three
# rows green: `chunk_node_at` losing its DO/LOOP arm, and the call op addressed
# at slot 0 rather than at its own slot. Both leave the ir arm printing nothing
# where the tree-walker prints "1" and "2".
program
trace i
zs = 'ab'
do zi = 1 to length(zs)
  say zi
end
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 2
err>      2 *-* zs = 'ab'
err>        >L>   "ab"
err>        >>>   "ab"
err>        >=>   ZS <= "ab"
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to length(zs)
err>        >L>   "1"
err>        >V>   ZS => "ab"
err>        >A>   "ab"
err>        >F>   LENGTH => "2"
err>        >K>   "TO" => "2"
err>        >=>   ZI <= "1"
err>      4 *-*   say zi
err>        >V>     ZI => "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      5 *-* end
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to length(zs)
err>        >V>     ZI => "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>        >>>     "2"
err>        >=>     ZI <= "2"
err>      4 *-*   say zi
err>        >V>     ZI => "2"
err>        >>>     "2"
err>      5 *-* end
err>      3 *-* do zi = 1 to length(zs)
err>        >V>     ZI => "2"
err>        >>>     "2"
err>        >>>     "3"
err>        >=>     ZI <= "3"

# trace i over a DO OVER ... FOR
#
# This row is the only trace-level record of a `DO OVER ... FOR` in this
# crate -- no other test or case file runs that shape under trace at all.
# Re-captured against the live oracle 2026-08-14 (`docs/superpowers/plans/
# 2026-08-13-over-for-keyword.md`): the count now carries its own
# `>K>   "FOR" => "1"`, immediately behind the `>L>   "1"` that evaluates it
# and ahead of the `>=>` that binds the control variable, the same position
# the OVER target's own `>K>` takes relative to its value.
program
trace i
zs = 'abc'
do qq over zs for 1
  say qq
end
